SciML and NeuralPDE look very interesting, but can't yet deal with complex geometries:

https://github.com/SciML/PDEBase.jl/issues/5

https://discourse.julialang.org/t/advanced-tutorials-pinns/80595/4

However, it can deal with integro-differential equations:

https://docs.sciml.ai/NeuralPDE/stable/tutorials/integro_diff/


gridap is a good option too, because of its cool gmsh support. Can it handle integro-differential equations?

See: https://matrix.to/#/!mSZoaZwNZhWulNruaK:gitter.im/$FIXU0459RksHEDaJgW4-MHgT87xJTPleTa3XZDByazQ?via=gitter.im&via=matrix.org&via=tu-dresden.de

gridap can handle multiple fields (coupled PDEs), so that' helpful. The geostropy + advection/diffusion problem can be written using multiple equations instead of a single integro-differential equation.

What about inflow/outflow conditons?  This isn't very obvious.

Gridap tutorials 4, 6, 8, 25 are useful.

Adding Coriolis term to the PDE breaks the variational property, so that a Lagrangian no longer exists. See: https://lab.ai.jh.edu/c/78c462f0-ea04-46f1-b663-4961c838c5e7

However, the weak form can be written down including the constraint of incompressibility using a Lagrange multiplier.

Build gridap Stokes solver OK with a stress boundary condition. See: TestingNavierStokes{4,4b}.ipynb. 

But adding Coriolis forces makes the solver run seemingly indefinitely, even for very weak Coriolis effect. Why?

GPT-4.1 says "Even a tiny Coriolis term makes your discrete system nearly singular unless your solver and preconditioning handle the new coupling structure. As a result, Newton or linear solve convergence rates collapse, and the solver may “freeze” or take huge numbers of steps."

Also: "The loss of symmetry in the rotating Stokes problem directly reflects the fact that the Coriolis term cannot be derived from a real scalar Lagrangian. For scalar Lagrangians, the equations they produce are always symmetric in the sense of the weak form. Any skew-symmetric (rotation-like) term destroys this, so no real minimization principle (Lagrangian) exists for the full system."

And: "The nonlinear convection term (u⋅∇)u breaks symmetry in the weak form, just like the Coriolis term, and precludes the existence of a real scalar Lagrangian for the full Navier-Stokes problem."

GridapSolvers.jl is useful, e.g., https://gridap.github.io/GridapSolvers.jl/stable/Examples/NavierStokes/

Many struggles with Gmsh and trying to build my ArcticBasin geometry. See files in Development_dont_delete directory.
The problem is either (i) Gmsh errors when I try to build the 3D mesh with a surface split into two physical pieces, or (ii) GridapGmsh errors when reading the .msh file.
The https://gitlab.onelab.info/gmsh/gmsh/-/blob/master/examples/boolean/cylinder_with_hole.geo example proves to be (finally) very useful.

Start working with nuPGCM instead of gridap directly.
